1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,383
Vehicles, equipment, facilities: every service operation runs on handovers.

2
00:00:05,683 --> 00:00:10,273
A technician records a finding, then the shift changes, and the detail is lost.

3
00:00:10,573 --> 00:00:14,329
Managers chase answers across notes, calls and systems.

4
00:00:14,629 --> 00:00:18,615
Hours go to asking, searching and re-keying, not doing the work.

5
00:00:18,915 --> 00:00:24,204
So critical issues stay buried until they cause delays, rework or safety incidents.

6
00:00:25,004 --> 00:00:28,337
Here is the solution, powered by Deloitte and NVIDIA.

7
00:00:29,137 --> 00:00:31,965
This is where the Service Operations Agent comes in.

8
00:00:32,265 --> 00:00:36,162
One live view of every job, shown here in automotive service.

9
00:00:36,462 --> 00:00:40,309
Right now, 12 vehicles are flagged as unsafe to release.

10
00:00:41,409 --> 00:00:45,575
Here is how it is built. A technician speaks or types an update.

11
00:00:45,875 --> 00:00:50,094
NVIDIA Riva transcribes it, and Nemotron extracts the facts.

12
00:00:50,394 --> 00:00:53,685
Code verifies every fact. Nothing is guessed.

13
00:00:53,985 --> 00:00:56,025
Each update joins an audit trail.

14
00:00:56,325 --> 00:00:59,476
Managers simply ask, and receive cited answers.

15
00:01:00,576 --> 00:01:05,116
Let us follow one update live. A technician dictates it from the bay.

16
00:01:05,416 --> 00:01:08,054
NVIDIA Riva turns the speech into text.

17
00:01:08,354 --> 00:01:12,716
Nemotron extracts the facts: the measurement and the specification.

18
00:01:13,616 --> 00:01:18,256
Code verifies it, and flags the repair order unsafe to release, instantly.

19
00:01:19,656 --> 00:01:23,463
With the update recorded, a manager asks the question that matters.

20
00:01:23,763 --> 00:01:26,637
Which vehicles cannot be released on safety grounds?

21
00:01:26,937 --> 00:01:31,808
13 vehicles, each one cited, in a tenth of a second.

22
00:01:32,508 --> 00:01:37,139
Then an open question: has anyone seen a whistling noise on a Passat?

23
00:01:37,439 --> 00:01:42,071
Search, reranking and a cited answer, in seconds, on the same GPU.

24
00:01:43,371 --> 00:01:47,355
The business impact: unsafe vehicles surface immediately.

25
00:01:47,655 --> 00:01:50,110
Managers get answers in a tenth of a second.

26
00:01:50,410 --> 00:01:52,869
Every figure traces back to a real record.

27
00:01:53,169 --> 00:01:57,112
And one update feeds the record, the handover and the dashboard.

28
00:01:57,912 --> 00:02:03,662
Behind it, the deployed architecture: three NVIDIA NIMs on one L40S.

29
00:02:04,362 --> 00:02:08,817
Riva handles speech. NeMo Guardrails stops unsafe requests.

30
00:02:09,117 --> 00:02:13,107
NeMo Evaluator scores every release. That is the whole stack.

31
00:02:14,907 --> 00:02:20,474
And it is all observable: DCGM and the app's metrics, live in Grafana.

32
00:02:21,574 --> 00:02:27,095
In summary, safety comes first: unsafe vehicles are flagged as updates are logged.

33
00:02:27,395 --> 00:02:33,290
Answers in a tenth of a second, every figure traced to a record, all on one GPU you own.

34
00:02:33,590 --> 00:02:38,973
Fast. Grounded. Yours to run. Shown in automotive, built for service operations.

